 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets deliver committed changes.** Revisions are pushed to browsers over socket connections this project operates, with replay from the change store for anything a browser missed. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected: it is the cheapest to operate but spends repeated reads on an unchanged board and leaves several seconds of lag on every update. A hosted realtime service was rejected: it would remove most connection operations, but costs recurring fees and couples the delivery path to one provider's subscription model. Self-hosting accepts connection-capacity and reconnect operations as the price of low-latency updates with no vendor in the path.
